1
01:00:03,733 --> 01:00:06,802
<b>Eugene Wigner, a Nobel Prize winning physicist,</b>

2
01:00:07,469 --> 01:00:10,073
<b>asked why mathematics describes the physical</b>

3
01:00:10,139 --> 01:00:13,443
<b>world with such surprising success. He called</b>

4
01:00:13,509 --> 01:00:17,246
<b>this the unreasonable effectiveness of mathematics.</b>

5
01:00:18,147 --> 01:00:21,084
<b>In the predictive universe framework, the answer</b>

6
01:00:21,150 --> 01:00:24,754
<b>begins with prediction itself. A knowable world</b>

7
01:00:24,821 --> 01:00:27,290
<b>must offer patterns stable enough for finite</b>

8
01:00:27,357 --> 01:00:31,461
<b>systems to learn and use. To predict, a system</b>

9
01:00:31,527 --> 01:00:34,831
<b>has to separate possibilities, compare expectation</b>

10
01:00:34,897 --> 01:00:38,401
<b>with result, keep what works, correct what fails,</b>

11
01:00:39,068 --> 01:00:42,071
<b>and carry useful patterns forward. That</b>

12
01:00:42,138 --> 01:00:44,907
<b>same loop gives formal thought its basic shape,</b>

13
01:00:45,675 --> 01:00:48,311
<b>clear distinctions, consistency, testing and</b>

14
01:00:48,378 --> 01:00:51,714
<b>proof. Mathematics takes these moves to their</b>

15
01:00:51,781 --> 01:00:55,251
<b>cleanest form. This also explains the appeal of</b>

16
01:00:55,318 --> 01:00:58,521
<b>elegant mathematics. The strongest structures let</b>

17
01:00:58,588 --> 01:01:00,957
<b>many relations fit together in a compact,</b>

18
01:01:01,024 --> 01:01:04,394
<b>reliable way. They hold order in a small form.</b>

19
01:01:05,428 --> 01:01:07,730
<b>Physics adds a harder test. Can the</b>

20
01:01:07,797 --> 01:01:10,233
<b>structure make a measurable difference for a real,</b>

21
01:01:10,299 --> 01:01:13,603
<b>finite predictor? Can it guide interaction,</b>

22
01:01:14,203 --> 01:01:18,174
<b>leave records, survive checks, and be maintained at</b>

23
01:01:18,241 --> 01:01:22,478
<b>finite cost? If not, it may remain valid</b>

24
01:01:22,545 --> 01:01:26,049
<b>mathematics, but in the predictive universe framework,</b>

25
01:01:26,516 --> 01:01:30,920
<b>it is physically idle. Mathematics works so well</b>

26
01:01:30,987 --> 01:01:34,123
<b>in physics because the same conditions that make</b>

27
01:01:34,190 --> 01:01:37,460
<b>prediction possible also shape the forms physical</b>

28
01:01:37,527 --> 01:01:41,497
<b>law can take. Mathematics maps the possible shapes</b>

29
01:01:41,564 --> 01:01:44,867
<b>of stable order. Physics keeps the shapes that</b>

30
01:01:44,934 --> 01:01:48,704
<b>can be realised, tested, and preserved in a resource</b>

31
01:01:48,805 --> 01:01:52,642
<b>constrained world. The mystery reduces to one</b>

32
01:01:52,709 --> 01:01:56,112
<b>deeper point. A knowable world must have stable</b>

33
01:01:56,179 --> 01:02:00,183
<b>predictive structure. Mathematics is the abstract</b>

34
01:02:00,249 --> 01:02:04,187
<b>grammar of that structure. Physics is its finite,</b>

35
01:02:04,754 --> 01:02:06,055
<b>testable realisation.</b>